1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,842
Stretch a rubber band.

2
00:00:01,842 --> 00:00:02,831
Pull a spring.

3
00:00:02,831 --> 00:00:04,319
Draw back a bow string.

4
00:00:04,719 --> 00:00:08,630
In all three, the further you
pull, the harder it pulls back.

5
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:11,472
Aarav stretches a rubber
band by ten centimetres.

6
00:00:11,472 --> 00:00:13,873
At the end, it pulls
back with twenty newtons.

7
00:00:14,223 --> 00:00:16,397
So how much work did he do?

8
00:00:16,397 --> 00:00:17,624
W equals F d.

9
00:00:17,624 --> 00:00:18,733
But which F?

10
00:00:18,733 --> 00:00:19,633
Zero?

11
00:00:19,336 --> 00:00:20,236
Twenty?

12
00:00:20,046 --> 00:00:21,424
It kept changing.

13
00:00:21,924 --> 00:00:24,624
So what do we do when
the force is not steady?

14
00:00:24,974 --> 00:00:27,462
Up to now, the force stayed the same.

15
00:00:27,462 --> 00:00:29,653
Work was just force times distance.

16
00:00:30,053 --> 00:00:34,277
On a force and distance graph,
a steady force is a flat line.

17
00:00:34,277 --> 00:00:38,177
Force times distance is the
area of the rectangle under it.

18
00:00:38,577 --> 00:00:41,519
When the force changes,
the line is not flat.

19
00:00:41,519 --> 00:00:42,868
But the idea stays.

20
00:00:42,868 --> 00:00:45,716
Work is still the area
under the force graph.

21
00:00:46,366 --> 00:00:47,599
Let's test it live.

22
00:00:47,599 --> 00:00:50,456
Aarav stretches the band
ten centimetres, slowly.

23
00:00:50,806 --> 00:00:52,738
For this band, the pull grows evenly.

24
00:00:52,738 --> 00:00:54,434
At five centimetres, ten newtons.

25
00:00:54,434 --> 00:00:55,865
At ten centimetres, twenty.

26
00:00:56,265 --> 00:00:58,516
Now cut the stretch into four steps.

27
00:00:58,516 --> 00:01:01,196
In each step, use the force at its start.

28
00:01:01,196 --> 00:01:03,675
Four strips give point seven five joules.

29
00:01:04,075 --> 00:01:06,587
Ten thinner steps give point nine joules.

30
00:01:06,587 --> 00:01:08,859
Forty steps, point nine seven five.

31
00:01:09,259 --> 00:01:11,975
The thinner the strips, the closer we get.

32
00:01:11,975 --> 00:01:14,449
The answer settles at exactly one joule.

33
00:01:14,949 --> 00:01:16,876
Slow-mo replay, one strip.

34
00:01:17,226 --> 00:01:21,090
Over a tiny step, delta x,
the force hardly changes.

35
00:01:21,090 --> 00:01:23,503
So the work is F times delta x.

36
00:01:23,503 --> 00:01:25,356
That is the strip's area.

37
00:01:25,856 --> 00:01:29,049
On the graph, the strips fill
the space under the line.

38
00:01:29,049 --> 00:01:30,535
Their total is the work.

39
00:01:31,185 --> 00:01:33,593
Now the formula, one step at a time.

40
00:01:33,943 --> 00:01:34,944
Step one.

41
00:01:34,944 --> 00:01:38,774
Over a tiny step, the
force is nearly steady.

42
00:01:38,774 --> 00:01:42,117
Delta W equals F of x, times delta x.

43
00:01:42,867 --> 00:01:43,778
Step two.

44
00:01:43,778 --> 00:01:45,377
Add all the strips.

45
00:01:45,377 --> 00:01:47,633
W is the sum of F delta x.

46
00:01:48,383 --> 00:01:49,283
Step three.

47
00:01:49,272 --> 00:01:53,291
Make the strips thinner and
thinner, and the sum becomes exact.

48
00:01:53,291 --> 00:01:55,842
We write it with a long S, for sum.

49
00:01:55,842 --> 00:01:58,045
W equals the integral of F d x.

50
00:01:58,795 --> 00:01:59,695
Step four.

51
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:04,220
On a graph, that is the area under
the force curve, from start to end.

52
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:06,354
Newtons times metres, so joules.

53
00:02:07,104 --> 00:02:08,083
Remember this.

54
00:02:08,083 --> 00:02:12,467
When the force keeps changing, work is
the area under the force and distance

55
00:02:12,467 --> 00:02:13,367
graph.

56
00:02:13,725 --> 00:02:15,048
Now, side by side.

57
00:02:15,048 --> 00:02:18,964
Two pulls, both from zero to twenty
newtons, over ten centimetres.

58
00:02:19,314 --> 00:02:22,321
Pull A grows fast at
first, then levels off.

59
00:02:22,321 --> 00:02:23,770
Its curve bulges up.

60
00:02:24,170 --> 00:02:27,196
Pull B stays small, then
shoots up at the end.

61
00:02:27,196 --> 00:02:28,537
Its curve sags low.

62
00:02:29,137 --> 00:02:30,037
Now you try.

63
00:02:30,011 --> 00:02:32,158
Which pull does more work, A or B?

64
00:02:32,158 --> 00:02:33,202
Pause and think.

65
00:02:36,402 --> 00:02:37,302
A!

66
00:02:36,747 --> 00:02:38,697
It has more area under its curve.

67
00:02:38,697 --> 00:02:40,940
Same start, same end, but more work.

68
00:02:41,590 --> 00:02:44,729
What if the force points
backward for part of the way?

69
00:02:44,729 --> 00:02:45,841
Follow the links.

70
00:02:46,191 --> 00:02:47,091
One.

71
00:02:46,670 --> 00:02:48,845
A backward force is a negative F.

72
00:02:49,245 --> 00:02:50,145
Two.

73
00:02:49,716 --> 00:02:52,183
Its strips hang below the distance axis.

74
00:02:52,583 --> 00:02:53,483
Three.

75
00:02:53,110 --> 00:02:56,352
A negative force times a forward
step gives negative work.

76
00:02:56,752 --> 00:02:57,652
Four.

77
00:02:57,323 --> 00:03:01,952
So the total work is the area above
the axis, minus the area below.

78
00:03:02,602 --> 00:03:04,292
Now, one worked example.

79
00:03:04,642 --> 00:03:08,704
A force grows from zero to ten
newtons over the first two metres.

80
00:03:08,704 --> 00:03:13,554
It stays at ten newtons up to five
metres, then falls to zero at six metres.

81
00:03:13,554 --> 00:03:14,626
Find the work.

82
00:03:15,126 --> 00:03:16,315
Split the area.

83
00:03:16,315 --> 00:03:19,720
The first triangle: half,
times two, times ten.

84
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:20,647
Ten joules.

85
00:03:21,397 --> 00:03:24,171
The rectangle: three
metres times ten newtons.

86
00:03:24,171 --> 00:03:25,138
Thirty joules.

87
00:03:25,888 --> 00:03:29,265
The last triangle: half,
times one, times ten.

88
00:03:29,265 --> 00:03:30,248
Five joules.

89
00:03:30,998 --> 00:03:33,169
Total area, forty five joules.

90
00:03:33,169 --> 00:03:34,521
That is the work.

91
00:03:35,171 --> 00:03:36,633
Now, the exam trap.

92
00:03:36,983 --> 00:03:42,025
For F equals three x squared, from zero
to two metres, many students average

93
00:03:42,025 --> 00:03:43,241
the end forces.

94
00:03:43,241 --> 00:03:45,097
Zero and twelve make six.

95
00:03:45,097 --> 00:03:47,262
Six times two, twelve joules.

96
00:03:47,612 --> 00:03:50,665
But averaging works only
for a straight line.

97
00:03:50,665 --> 00:03:53,841
The real area is x
cubed, from zero to two.

98
00:03:53,841 --> 00:03:54,886
Eight joules.

99
00:03:55,536 --> 00:03:57,089
Where is this asked?

100
00:03:57,089 --> 00:04:01,644
Reading work as area from force and
distance graphs, and integrating a

101
00:04:01,644 --> 00:04:02,655
given force.

102
00:04:03,255 --> 00:04:04,463
Exam level now.

103
00:04:04,463 --> 00:04:08,433
A force F equals five minus
two x newtons acts on a body.

104
00:04:08,433 --> 00:04:11,390
It moves along x from zero to four metres.

105
00:04:11,390 --> 00:04:12,863
Find the work done.

106
00:04:12,863 --> 00:04:14,122
Pause and solve.

107
00:04:19,522 --> 00:04:20,944
The answer is A.

108
00:04:20,944 --> 00:04:22,013
Four joules.

109
00:04:22,513 --> 00:04:27,165
W is the integral of five
minus two x, from zero to four.

110
00:04:27,165 --> 00:04:29,761
That is five x minus x squared.

111
00:04:30,261 --> 00:04:32,722
At four metres, twenty minus sixteen.

112
00:04:32,722 --> 00:04:33,905
At zero, zero.

113
00:04:33,905 --> 00:04:35,040
So four joules.

114
00:04:35,540 --> 00:04:40,149
On the graph, six point two five joules
sit above the axis, and two point

115
00:04:40,149 --> 00:04:41,282
two five below.

116
00:04:41,282 --> 00:04:42,857
The difference is four.

117
00:04:43,457 --> 00:04:44,997
Here is your recap.

118
00:04:44,997 --> 00:04:46,580
Screenshot this card.

119
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:51,966
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:04:52,366 --> 00:04:53,266
One.

121
00:04:52,837 --> 00:04:56,573
Why can you not just use W
equals F d for a stretched band?

122
00:04:57,473 --> 00:04:58,373
Two.

123
00:04:57,893 --> 00:05:01,199
What does the area under a force
and distance graph tell you?

124
00:05:02,099 --> 00:05:02,999
Three.

125
00:05:02,632 --> 00:05:05,192
What does area below
the distance axis mean?

126
00:05:06,292 --> 00:05:10,233
Next time: a slow ball taps
the pads, a fast ball stings.

127
00:05:10,233 --> 00:05:12,351
Where does the sting come from?

128
00:05:12,351 --> 00:05:14,419
We find out in kinetic energy.
